<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Emperor Taizong of Jin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1075">1075</a>–<a href="/years/1135">1135</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Wanyan Wuqimai, Wanyan Sheng</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Emperor Taizong of Jin, Wanyan Wuqimai, was the second emperor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchen-jin-dynasty">Jurchen Jin dynasty</a>, ruling from 1123 to 1135 after his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-taizu-of-jin">Aguda</a>. In 1125 his armies captured the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao</a> emperor, Tianzuo, ending the Liao state. He then turned on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a>, whose capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaifeng">Kaifeng</a> fell in the Jingkang Incident of 1127, bringing most of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> under Jin rule.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/emperor-taizong-of-jin.jpg" alt="Emperor Taizong of Jin" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Slb nsk · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wanyan_Wuqimai.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
